<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
	<PropertyGroup>
		<TargetFrameworks>netstandard2.0;net10.0</TargetFrameworks>
		<RootNamespace>DotGram.Finance</RootNamespace>
		<Description>FIX 4.4 tag-value parsing built with DotGram.</Description>

		<PackageReleaseNotes>0.2.0 replaces the public surface. This is what to write instead, line by line; everything below was read from the published 0.1.0 assembly rather than remembered. THE ENTRY POINTS ARE NEW. 0.1.0 had one, Fix44, with eight methods. Fix44.Parse(wire) and Fix44.Parse(wire, FixParseMode.Strict) -> FixMessages.Parse(wire) followed by message.Validate(), which answers with everything wrong rather than throwing on the first. Fix44.Parse(wire, FixParseMode.Lenient) -> FixMessages.Parse(wire), which is now what parsing means. Fix44.TryParse(wire, out m, out e, mode) -> FixMessages.TryParse(wire, out m, out e). And there is a layer below it that 0.1.0 did not have: FixParser reads flat typed fields from a string, a span, a byte array, a TextReader or a Stream, without assembling a message. THE TYPES YOU NAME. NewOrderSingle, ExecutionReport and the other 91 message classes -> FixMessage.NewOrderSingle and so on: they are nested in FixMessage now, a closed set whose base stands in front of every arm of a switch, which is how this package writes its field hierarchy too. CustomFixMessage -> FixMessage.Custom, since a nested case does not repeat the name it is nested in. A message's own properties are untouched: text is still string, numbers are still FixNumber?, and Header, Trailer, AllFields, OriginalWire, GetField and GetGroup keep their names and their meaning. What changes under them is two type names. The 81 typed group-entry classes (PartiesEntry, AllocsEntry and the rest) are withdrawn, so order.Parties is an IReadOnlyList&lt;FixFieldSet&gt; read with GetField and GetGroup rather than a list of a class per group. And the field view is renamed: 0.1.0's FixField -- the readonly struct with Tag, Value, Wire, Position, ValuePosition, Length, TryGetDecimal and TryGetInt64 -- is now FixFieldView, member for member, with TypedValue added. The name FixField is reused for something else: the closed set of typed field cases the new field layer returns (FixField.Custom, FixField.RawData, FixField.Symbol and the rest), which is what FixFieldView.TypedValue hands you. THE OPTIONS. new FixParseOptions(mode, pairs) -> new FixFieldOptions(pairs), and the pairs are an IReadOnlyDictionary&lt;int,int&gt; from length tag to data tag rather than a FixDataPair[]. A supplied dictionary now ADDS to the standard's sixteen pairs instead of replacing them, so a dictionary that repeated a standard pair to keep it is refused with the tag named, and one that meant to add can drop everything it was repeating. FixParseMode is gone, and with it FixParseOptions.Mode: reading the wire and holding it to a schema are two acts. Parse builds whatever it can read; message.Validate() reports everything wrong at once, each finding naming its rule, its scope, its tag, the entry of the repeating group it is in, and where in the source it begins. So a message Strict used to turn away is now returned and reported on: a required field absent, a required component empty, a value outside its type or code set, a tag twice in one scope, a tag the schema does not place in that scope, a group's fields out of order, an unknown MsgType, and MessageEncoding absent beside an Encoded field. What still refuses is what the reader cannot defer: framing, BodyLength, CheckSum, a field it could not read, a length/data pair that does not measure, a NumInGroup that sizes an array past the fields left, and a group entry that does not begin with its delimiter. LOG FRAMING IS A VALUE. There is no ParseLog on either layer: ParseLog(text) -> Parse(text, FixFieldOptions.Log), and options.With(FixFraming.Log) reads your own dictionary in log framing. Which separator an input uses is a property of the input. OPTIONS ARE AN OPTIONAL ARGUMENT, not a second method, as FixParser has always had them, so FixMessages has twelve methods where the pair-per-setting shape would have twenty-three. Two call shapes change silently rather than failing to compile: Parse(reader, 4096) now binds 4096 to nothing and must become Parse(reader, null, 4096), and TryParse(input, out m, out e, null) once threw and now reads with the defaults. WHAT IS NEW BESIDES. A streamed call takes bufferSize and maxRetained, and a field past the limit is an IOException in both the generated and the hand parser. Numbers are decimal. Every public member is documented and a package skill ships beside the README. FixFieldOptions takes a FixCustomFields, which builds the fields of tags the package does not define; it builds field objects only, and leaves the tag unknown to the message schema, so Validate still reports it. message.Validate(validator) checks against a schema of your own: FixDictionary.Load reads a counterparty's QuickFIX XML dictionary, and FixValidator is a table of rules you may write a message type at a time. </PackageReleaseNotes>
		<PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
		<PackageTags>fix;finance;trading;parser;dotgram</PackageTags>

		<!-- The package ships its XML documentation, and every public member has some:
		     under TreatWarningsAsErrors a member left undocumented fails the build. -->
		<GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>

		<DebugType>embedded</DebugType>
		<ContinuousIntegrationBuild Condition="'$(GITHUB_ACTIONS)' == 'true'">true</ContinuousIntegrationBuild>
		<EmitCompilerGeneratedFiles>true</EmitCompilerGeneratedFiles>
		<CompilerGeneratedFilesOutputPath>$(BaseIntermediateOutputPath)GeneratedFiles</CompilerGeneratedFilesOutputPath>
	</PropertyGroup>
	<ItemGroup>
		<InternalsVisibleTo Include="DotGram.Finance.Tests" />
		<InternalsVisibleTo Include="DotGram.Handwritten" />
		<InternalsVisibleTo Include="DotGram.Finance.Fix44" />
		<InternalsVisibleTo Include="DotGram.Finance.Fix44.Tests" />

		<ProjectReference Include="../DotGram/DotGram.csproj" OutputItemType="Analyzer" ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" />

		<AdditionalFiles Include="Fix/*.gram" />

		<None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="/" />

		<!-- The skill beside it, so that an agent working in a project that has restored
		     this package can read how to use it without leaving the machine. The README
		     says where it is, in a comment nothing renders. -->
		<None Include="SKILL.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="/" />

		<None Include="../../LICENSE" Pack="true" PackagePath="licenses/DotGram/" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup Condition="'$(TargetFramework)' == 'netstandard2.0'">
		<PackageReference Include="System.Memory" />
		<PackageReference Include="Meziantou.Polyfill" PrivateAssets="all" />
	</ItemGroup>
</Project>
